## Effect Metric Menagerie {.smaller background-image="images/menagerie.jpg" background-size="cover" background-opacity="50%"}


::: {.notes}

- Effect size metric is a fundamental assumption of meta-analysis

- Choice of metric is constrained by

    - Studies designs

    - Data availability, reporting conventions

    - Heterogeneity of study features (e.g., outcome scales)

- But in many applications, __*more than one metric is often viable*__.
:::

::::: {.columns}

:::: {.column width="48%" .redbox .fragment}


#### Group comparison of binary outcomes

- Risk differences $\pi_1 - \pi_0$
- Risk ratios (log-transformed) $\log\left(\frac{\pi_1}{\pi_0}\right)$
- Odds ratios (log-transformed) $\log\left(\frac{\pi_1 / (1 - \pi_1)}{\pi_0 / (1 - \pi_0)}\right)$
- Bivariate models for $\pi_0, \pi_1$

::::

:::: {.column width="48%" .purplebox .fragment}

#### Group comparison of continuous outcomes

- Raw mean differences $\mu_1 - \mu_0$
- Standardized mean differences $\delta = \frac{\mu_1 - \mu_0}{\sigma}$
- Percentage of maximum possible (POMP) differences
- Response ratios (log-transformed) $\lambda = \log\left(\frac{\mu_1}{\mu_0}\right)$
- Probability of superiority

::::

:::::

::::: {.columns}

:::: {.column width="48%" .greenbox .fragment}

#### Single-group prevalence

- Raw proportions $\pi$
- Arcsine-transformation $a = \text{asin}\left(\sqrt{\pi}\right)$
- Freeman-Tukey double-arcsine transformation

:::: 

:::: {.column width="48%" .bluebox .fragment}

#### Bivariate associations / psychometric

- Pearson's correlation $\rho$
- Fisher's $z$-transformation $\zeta = \text{atanh}(\rho)$
- Reliability-corrected correlation $\frac{\rho_{xy}}{\sqrt{\rho_{xx}\rho_{yy}}}$

::::

::::: 


## Metric choice methodology {.smaller}

- Large literature on effect metrics for group comparison on binary outcomes.

    - Theoretical arguments about interpretability, stability, non-collapsibility [@Poole2015risk; @Panagiotou2015commentary].

    - Risk differences tend to be more heterogeneous [@Engels2000heterogeneity; @Zhao2022empirical].

::: {.fragment}
- Strong opinions about effect metrics for group comparison on continuous outcomes [@Cummings2011arguments].

    - Some novel alternatives to avoid standarization [@Ades2015simultaneous; @Lu2014simultaneous; @Downing2025synthesis].

    - Various methods for standardization [e.g., @Hopkins2024standardization; @Fitzgerald2025using].

:::

::: {.fragment}
- Choice between standardized mean difference and response ratio metrics

    - Sensitivity analyses using both metrics [@Friedrich2011ratio].

    - Model both metrics simultaneously [@Yang2024bivariate].

:::

::: {.fragment }

### But is there any way to evaluate effect metric choice _as a model assumption_?

:::

## Use predictive fit to inform metric choice

- Evaluate effect metrics by performance in __predicting summary data for a new study__.

    - Data vector $\mathbf{d}_i$ consisting of summary statistics used to compute effect size estimates.

::: {.fragment}

- Use leave-one-out log predictive density (LOO-LPD) to measure predictive performance.
  $$
  \text{LOO-LPD} = \frac{1}{k} \sum_{i=1}^{k} \log p\left(\mathbf{d}_i \left| \hat\mu_{(-i)}, \hat\tau_{(-i)}, \mathbf{X}_i, N_i\right.\right)
  $$

:::

## Leave-one-out predictive density {.smaller}

:::: {.columns}

::: {.column width="40%"}
- Without using observation $i$, make a prediction for observation $i$ by specifying a density $p_{(-i)}(x_i)$ (must integrate to one).

:::

::: {.column width="60%"}


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](index_files/figure-revealjs/unnamed-chunk-1-1.png){width=90%}
:::
:::


:::
::::

:::: {.columns .fragment}

::: {.column width="40%"}

- Score higher by putting more density on the realized outcome.

$$
\text{Predictive score:} \ \log p_{(-i)}(x_i)
$$

:::

::: {.column width="60%"}


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](index_files/figure-revealjs/unnamed-chunk-2-1.png){width=90%}
:::
:::


:::
::::

::: {.fragment}

- LOO-LPD is the sum of scores across $N$ observations: $\displaystyle{\text{LOO-LPD} = \sum_{i=1}^N \log p_{(-i)}(x_i)}$

  - Higher LOO-LPD indicates better fit.

  - Agnostic to the metric of the prediction.

:::


## Predicting summary data

- Many effect size metrics are __functions of multiple parameters__.

    - Group comparisons of binary outcomes are functions of $\pi_0,\pi_1$

    - Group comparisons of continuous outcomes are functions of $\mu_0,\mu_1,\sigma$

    - Reliability-corrected correlations are functions of $\rho_{xy},\rho_{xx},\rho_{xy}$.

::: {.fragment}

- Problem:

  - Meta-analysis models for such metrics are __*not sufficiently generative for predicting the data vector*__ $\mathbf{d}_i$.

- Solution:

  - Augment the meta-analysis model with __an auxiliary model__ for some of the parameters.

:::


## Effectiveness of nicotine replacement therapy {.smaller}


::: {.cell}

:::


- Cochrane Systematic Review of effects of nicotine replacement therapy vs. control on smoking cessation, defined as abstinence at 6+ month follow-up [@HartmannBoyce2018nicotine].

- Sample sizes ranging from $N_i$ = 36 to 5290 (median = 240.5, IQR = 153.5 - 428.5).

